  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Cool weather and periodic showers and \nthunderstorms, some of the lake-effect variety, are expected \nthrough Tuesday.\n\nSurface low pressure near far SW Quebec this early morning will \nslowly fill as it lifts northeastward today, while aloft a mid/upper \nlevel closed low will pass across our region. Two noted short-wave \ntroughs, one near Lake Erie this morning, and a second near the \neastern waters of Lake Ontario this afternoon will bring added lift \nto enhance showers and isolated thunderstorms. \n\nUnder this upper level low, 850 hPa temperatures will lower to \naround +7C that with plenty of clouds around will leave temperatures \nthrough the afternoon in the 60s...or about 10 to 15 degrees below \nnormal. \n\nThis cool airmass will also generate a fair amount of lake enhanced \nrain showers as well as diurnally driven showers and isolated \nthunderstorms. Initial showers and thunderstorms this morning near \nLake Erie and far WNY will diminish through the mid morning hours. \nAs daytime instability increases showers and a few thunderstorms \nwill increase inland, in addition to periodic lake enhanced batches \nof showers arriving near the lake shores off the Lakes. This \nsecondary shortwave will enhance the coverage area of convection \nthis afternoon towards the Finger Lakes and eastern Lake Ontario \nregion.  \n\nTonight, while inland convection will begin to wane, the still cool \ncyclonic flow over the warm lake waters will continue to generate \nperiodic showers off the warm lake waters. Deeper moisture will be \nfound over Lake Ontario, and will place higher PoPs, and greater \namount of QPF here tonight for continued lake effect rain showers, \nwhile scattered and lighter rain showers will be found off Lake \nErie. \n\nOn a WNW flow Tuesday rain showers will begin to taper off south and \nsoutheast of first Lake Erie and then Lake Ontario as deeper \nmoisture wanes. A few afternoon showers and perhaps a thunderstorm \nnear the exiting upper level low will remain east of Lake Ontario. \n\nKey Message 2...Dry weather expected region-wide this Tuesday night \nand Wednesday.\n\nBy Tuesday night a mid level shortwave ridge will replace the \nrecently departed upper level low, with a surface ridge of high \npressure expanding northeastward across our region from the Ohio \nValley. This will bring clearing skies Tuesday night, with some \nvalley fog across the Southern Tier/Black River Valley. The surface \nhigh pressure will crest over our region Wednesday, and with a light \nsoutheasterly flow afternoon temperatures will return back to normal \nunder a mix of sunshine a fair weather cumulus.",
